        "urpx:selfContained": true,
        "urpx:explainedBy": {
          "@type": "urpx:Evaluation",
          "@id": "jemc:cc-slms-2026-01-01-evaluation",
          "urpx:evaluationKind": {
            "@id": "urpx:coverageEvaluation"
          },
          "urpx:description": "This filing does not carry everything its source document states. 6 parts are deferred, listed here in the same words as the filing's tariff.toml [modeling].deferrals so the two records cannot drift. (1) Coincident Peak Billing Demand. For each calendar year's bills the Coincident Peak Billing Demand is the customer's average 60-minute demand at Georgia Transmission Corporation's 5 highest demands during peak period notification hours in the 12 months ending September 30 of the previous year, which the Cooperative estimates for customers not previously metered hourly. It is a calculated MetricInput whose rule is text in its CalculationMethod; Georgia Transmission's peak hours are published by neither the sheet nor the Cooperative, so a consumer has to obtain them to compute it. (2) Schools. The schedule is for state, county, city and private schools offering regular elementary, middle, high school or college courses. URPX v0.5.1 has no customer kind or sub-kind for a school, so the EligibilityRule carries non-residential customer kind and the condition is in its description only. (3) Wholesale Power Cost Adjustment factor. Schedule P-22 increases or decreases every bill by a per-kWh factor the Cooperative computes each month from its estimated purchased-power costs and sales, and publishes no factor. The JSON-LD defines the adjustment as a per-kWh PriceDefinition with no Price, so a modeled bill is high or low by the month's factor times its kWh. (4) Excess Reactive Demand. Where a power factor below 95 percent lagging is indicated, the Cooperative may install metering for reactive demand and bills kVAR above one-third of the measured kW at $0.30 per kVAR. The JSON-LD carries the $0.30 on a calculated MetricInput whose formula is text in its CalculationMethod; a consumer has to compute the excess kVAR, and supplies none for a customer the Cooperative does not meter for it. (5) Minimum Monthly Charge. The minimum monthly charge is the greater of two options: (1) the greater of $12.00 per kW of (a) the highest on-peak demand measured during the billing months June through September or (b) 65 percent of the highest demand measured during the billing months October through May or the summer off-peak demand, never less than the Service Charge plus excess kVAR charges; or (2) a charge specified in a supplemental contract. A minimum is a floor under the bill, which no Price expresses, so it is not carried; a modeled bill below it is low by the difference. (6) Terms of Payment and Contract Period. The sheet adds a late fee and disconnection charges under the Service Rules and Regulations, makes all charges subject to applicable taxes, and sets a one-year contract. None is priced on the sheet and none is carried.",
          "urpx:evaluatedOn": "2026-10-18T00:00:00"
        }
      }
    }
  }
